class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int>> generate(int numRows) {
        vector<vector<int>> triangle;
        for (int i = 0; i < numRows; ++i) {
            // Row i has i+1 elements
            vector<int> row(i + 1, 1);  // starting all with 1 
            for (int j = 1; j < i; ++j) {
                row[j] = triangle[i - 1][j - 1] + triangle[i - 1][j];
            }
            triangle.push_back(row);
        }
        return triangle;
    }
};

What the variables mean
triangle is a vector<vector<int>> storing all rows built so far.

i is the current row index we are building (0-based).

j is the current column index inside that row (also 0-based).

row is the new row we are constructing for index i.

So:

triangle[i - 1] = the previous row (the row just above the current one).

triangle[i - 1][j - 1] = element in the previous row, one position to the left.

triangle[i - 1][j] = element in the previous row, same column index.
